IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `fraud-nouns`
IMPORT `fraud-dates`
IMPORT `fraud-s15`
IMPORT `fraud-penalty`

-- Fraud Act 2006 (2006 c. 35), sections 6 to 8: ARTICLES FOR USE IN FRAUDS.
--
-- Section 6: having an article for use in fraud.  Section 7: making, adapting, supplying or
-- offering to supply an article.  Section 8: "article" includes any program or data held in
-- electronic form.
--
-- Section 6(1) states NO mental element: it asks only whether the article is "for use in the
-- course of or in connection with any fraud", and whose fraud that is the text does not say.  The
-- module follows the text and takes that as a fact the person states (fork F9 in NOTES.md); it
-- does not add a requirement of knowledge or intention that section 6 does not state.  Section
-- 7(1), by contrast, states two mental elements, either of which suffices.

§ `Articles for use in frauds (sections 6 to 8)`

§§ `Section 8: "article"`

-- src:67 | 8 “Article”
-- src:68 | (1) For the purposes of—
-- src:69 | (a) sections 6 and 7, and
-- src:70 | (b) the provisions listed in subsection (2), so far as they relate to articles for use in the course of or in connection with fraud,
-- src:71 | “article” includes any program or data held in electronic form.
-- src:72 | (2) The provisions are—
-- src:73 | (a) section 1(7)(b) of the Police and Criminal Evidence Act 1984 (c. 60),
-- src:74 | (b) section 2(8)(b) of the Armed Forces Act 2001 (c. 19), and
-- src:75 | (c) Article 3(7)(b) of the Police and Criminal Evidence (Northern Ireland) Order 1989 (S.I. 1989/1341 (N.I. 12));
-- src:76 | (meaning of “prohibited articles” for the purposes of stop and search powers).
@ref Fraud Act 2006 s 8(1) and (2) (src:68-76)
GIVEN `the thing` IS AN `Article case`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`section 8(1): the thing is an article for the purposes of sections 6 and 7` `the thing` MEANS
    -- "Article" includes any program or data held in electronic form.  "Includes" does not shrink the
    -- ordinary sense of the word, which the Act does not define and which is asked as a fact.
    -- Section 8(1)(b) gives the same extension to the stop and search provisions listed in
    -- section 8(2) (powers of constables, not of the person): those powers are not encoded.
        `the thing`'s `an article in the ordinary sense of the word`
    OR  `the thing`'s `a program held in electronic form`
    OR  `the thing`'s `data held in electronic form`

§§ `Section 6: possession etc. of articles for use in frauds`

-- src:53 | 6 Possession etc. of articles for use in frauds
-- src:54 | (1) A person is guilty of an offence if he has in his possession or under his control any article for use in the course of or in connection with any fraud.
@ref Fraud Act 2006 s 6(1) (src:54)
GIVEN `the case` IS A `Possession case`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`section 6(1): the elements of the offence are made out` `the case` MEANS
        (   `the case`'s `in his possession`
         OR `the case`'s `under his control`)
    AND `section 8(1): the thing is an article for the purposes of sections 6 and 7` (`the case`'s `the article`)
    AND `the case`'s `for use in the course of or in connection with any fraud`

-- src:55 | (2) A person guilty of an offence under this section is liable—
-- src:56 | (a) on summary conviction, to imprisonment for a term not exceeding the general limit in a magistrates’ court or to a fine not exceeding the statutory maximum (or to both);
-- src:57 | (b) on conviction on indictment, to imprisonment for a term not exceeding 5 years or to a fine (or to both).
@ref Fraud Act 2006 s 6(2) (src:55-57)
GIVEN place IS A Place
      `the date the offence was committed` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE Penalty
`section 6(2): the penalty for possession etc. of articles for use in frauds` place `the date the offence was committed` MEANS
    `the penalty subsection: what may follow conviction` 5 place `the date the offence was committed`

-- Section 6(3) (Northern Ireland reads the limit as 6 months) is stated once, with its four
-- twins, in fraud-penalty.l4.

§§ `Section 7: making or supplying articles for use in frauds`

-- src:59 | 7 Making or supplying articles for use in frauds
-- src:60 | (1) A person is guilty of an offence if he makes, adapts, supplies or offers to supply any article—
-- src:61 | (a) knowing that it is designed or adapted for use in the course of or in connection with fraud, or
-- src:62 | (b) intending it to be used to commit, or assist in the commission of, fraud.
@ref Fraud Act 2006 s 7(1) (src:60-62)
GIVEN `the case` IS A `Making or supplying case`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`section 7(1): the elements of the offence are made out` `the case` MEANS
        `section 7(1): makes, adapts, supplies or offers to supply` (`the case`'s `what the person does with it`)
    AND `section 8(1): the thing is an article for the purposes of sections 6 and 7` (`the case`'s `the article`)
    AND (   `the case`'s `knowing that it is designed or adapted for use in the course of or in connection with fraud`
         OR `the case`'s `intending it to be used to commit, or assist in the commission of, fraud`)

@ref Fraud Act 2006 s 7(1) (src:60)
GIVEN `what the person does with the article` IS A `Dealing with an article`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`section 7(1): makes, adapts, supplies or offers to supply` `what the person does with the article` MEANS
        `what the person does with the article` EQUALS `makes it`
    OR  `what the person does with the article` EQUALS `adapts it`
    OR  `what the person does with the article` EQUALS `supplies it`
    OR  `what the person does with the article` EQUALS `offers to supply it`

-- src:63 | (2) A person guilty of an offence under this section is liable—
-- src:64 | (a) on summary conviction, to imprisonment for a term not exceeding the general limit in a magistrates’ court or to a fine not exceeding the statutory maximum (or to both);
-- src:65 | (b) on conviction on indictment, to imprisonment for a term not exceeding 10 years or to a fine (or to both).
@ref Fraud Act 2006 s 7(2) (src:63-65)
GIVEN place IS A Place
      `the date the offence was committed` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE Penalty
`section 7(2): the penalty for making or supplying articles for use in frauds` place `the date the offence was committed` MEANS
    `the penalty subsection: what may follow conviction` 10 place `the date the offence was committed`

-- Section 7(3) (Northern Ireland reads the limit as 6 months) is stated once, with its four
-- twins, in fraud-penalty.l4.
